comment_data.body:
AI finding:

> this re-seeks from column 0 of the row for every word. `marktree_itr_get_filter(buf->b_marktree, csarg->virt_row, 0, ...)` then `inline_virt_text_width()` walks and discards every mark below the word start. That's O(words ×
  marks) per line, not the O(chars + marks in the word) the description implies, and it's not only drawing: `linesize_regular`/`getvcol`/`plines_win_col` walk the same line char by char.

suggestion:

Seek from the word start instead, or when `!skip_cur_text` just copy the already-positioned shared iterator.

```diff
-  MarkTreeIter virt_iter[1];
-  bool const has_virt = csarg->virt_row >= 0
-                        && marktree_itr_get_filter(buf->b_marktree, csarg->virt_row, 0,
-                                                   csarg->virt_row + 1, 0, inline_filter,
-                                                   virt_iter);
+  // Seek on first use, from the word start: the loop below often breaks on its first check
+  // (one-char words, end of line), and seeking from column 0 would rescan the whole line.
+  MarkTreeIter virt_iter[1];
+  int has_virt = csarg->virt_row < 0 ? 0 : -1;  // -1: not sought yet

   colnr_T vcol2 = vcol;
   while (true) {
     ...
     int const col = (int)(s - line);
+    if (has_virt < 0) {
+      has_virt = marktree_itr_get_filter(buf->b_marktree, csarg->virt_row, col,
+                                         csarg->virt_row + 1, 0, inline_filter, virt_iter);
+    }
```

